ol, and same-column support toggles. This removes most of the prior search branching without losing the verified mechanics.\n- The canonical action-7 model is correct but breadth remains too large: 500 states have not reached a new target component. I’m taking one more checkpoint, then I’ll inspect the frontier depth and switch to macro-level transitions if primitive BFS is still shallow.\n- The repeated-room ambiguity has a concrete cause: off-screen support states persist but are absent from the frame key, exactly as in level 6. I’m testing the staged 38-action pass by rerunning the opening, then descending the formerly lethal right shaft; if the hidden support was prepared correctly, that descent should now survive.\n- I’ve narrowed level 7 to a gravity-and-persistent-support puzzle. The key remaining check is whether the 38-step loop staged an off-screen support; I’m restoring and testing that exact state before changing the solver.\n- The loop does preserve two expanded supports, but its apparent “reset” is not the original state: the left-strip click is inert there, so gravity is already in a different control phase. I’m mapping that staged frame and testing both gravity actions directly rather than replaying the opening blindly.\n- The mechanics now separate cleanly: either coordinate action on a colour-8 cross flips gravity; action 6 expands a colour-12 support and action 7 contracts it. The 38-step loop ends with gravity up and a preserved support, so I’m testing the intended return descent by landing on, then retracting, the lower support.\n- The direct clone tree confirms the harness’s main constraint: nested clones become too expensive before even 100 states. I’m stopping that branch and switching to room-level probes, where each candidate is replayed from one shallow clone and each support decision is explicit.\n- A decisive mechanic is now verified: action 6 selects and toggles the clicked object; action 7 re-activates that selected object even after i